---
// Pricing (site-prelaunch.md §3) — copy verbatim from "Page: Pricing" (#41, FINAL).
// Monthly-only: no annual figures landed, so the designed billing toggle ships dormant
// (NOT built — zero JS, per the design's own conditional + PM annual-pricing decision).
// Connection-optional: works with or without a broker connection. Core's $49 is the
// Accepted price (pricing-artifact.md §1, human sign-off 2026-06-30), matching
// FastSpring's catalog exactly. Tier symmetry is a compliance feature (spec §6.2):
// never differentiate.
// Free card (2026-07-22, stock-analyst-platform#1307): shipped decoupled from the
// trial-launch reveal (consult 0182, ADR 0145 item 6) — Free needs neither ORATS (#97)
// nor a live checkout, so it shipped alone, ahead of its CTA.
// CTAs lit (2026-08-22, stock-analyst-platform#2513/#2514) — BOTH cards, one change,
// because §6.2 makes a CTA on one card and none on the other the asymmetry the rule
// exists to prevent. This REPLACES this comment's own previous claim that "the chooser
// block and Core's trial CTA stay gated on #97/#31": both halves of that gate are dead.
// #31 closed `not_planned` (the pre-Dodo MoR application), and ADR 0560 item 3 superseded
// the #97 half on 2026-08-02 — "The Core trial offer is un-gated from ORATS… Core is
// offered at go-live" — because structure math is broker-fed, not vendor-fed. What
// actually gated the CTAs was live MoR checkout (#1459/#201/#1458, all closed) and the
// Core card's own unserved-layer claim (#2519, closed 2026-08-22).
// The chooser block remains unbuilt — it is separate copy, not part of #2513/#2514.
// Labels differ per site-copy-twiceover.md §Pricing (signed off); the CTA *treatment* is
// identical, which is what §6.2 and site-prelaunch.md §3's "equal weight" govern.
// The paid cards' two sub-lines are the negative-option disclosure (ADR 0145, FTC/EU-UK
// auto-renewal) — verbatim, legal-flagged; they are not decoration and never ship
// without the CTA they qualify, nor it without them. Premium's are Core's with $99 for $49:
// ADR 0145 Amendment 9 gives Premium the same 14-day, card-required, auto-charge trial.
// THREE PLANS (stock-analyst-platform#3829; ADR 0912 Amendment 1, build reference
// ai-team/design/assets/pricing-page-c4-three-plans-2026-09-24.html, #3962; copy filled by
// Growth on #3693). This supersedes the page's two-card history — the Premium card held out
// (#1766/#1767), and the per-card feature lists that were narrowed and re-derived against
// live flag state (#2519, #2544/#2567, #2822). That history is in git; what replaced it:
//   - Each card carries ONE plan line, the homepage strip's own, verbatim (ruling 1). The
//     feature lists drifted; the plans now differ only in which sections they include, and
//     the section table below says that once.
//   - The table renders from src/data/sections.mjs, the site's one section list (ruling 2,
//     ADR 0990), so its rows, captions and 6 / 10 / 13 totals cannot disagree with the band.
//   - A subgrid keeps the three CTAs level from 860px (ruling 3); below that the cards stack.
//   - Premium's CTA is /go/plan-premium, not the reference's `/go/plan?tier=premium`: the
//     forwarder drops unnamed params, so that URL would have opened Core's trial.
// The caps are figures in the card's figure block (ADR 0912, #3549), set to 10 / 200 / 300
// by ADR 0960 (#3932). The homepage strip's captions and plan lines quote these cards;
// pricing-strip-and-closing-band.test.mjs asserts both equalities from both surfaces and
// pins the three numbers so neither page can drift, nor both together.
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { PLANS, sectionGroups, sectionsOn } from "../data/sections.mjs";

// Offer JSON-LD (SEO audit 2026-07 finding 7) — the Accepted prices, verbatim from
// pricing-artifact.md §1 (human sign-off 2026-06-30). No numbers invented here.
// Premium joins with its card (#3829) — a structured-data Offer advertises a plan as much as
// the visible card does, so the two appear and go together.
// url/availability/per-offer url (SEO re-audit 2026-08-23, stock-analyst-platform#2634): the site is
// trailingSlash: "never", so the old "/pricing/" 307-redirected to the real canonical — dropped the
// trailing slash. Each offer now also carries its own url (the live CTA it's actually redeemed
// through, already on the page below) and availability, per Google's Offer recommendations.
const productJsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Product",
  name: "TwiceOver",
  url: "https://twiceover.io/pricing",
  offers: [
    {
      "@type": "Offer",
      name: "Free",
      price: "0",
      priceCurrency: "USD",
      availability: "https://schema.org/InStock",
      url: "https://twiceover.io/go/try",
    },
    {
      "@type": "Offer",
      name: "Core",
      price: "49",
      priceCurrency: "USD",
      availability: "https://schema.org/InStock",
      url: "https://twiceover.io/go/plan",
    },
    {
      "@type": "Offer",
      name: "Premium",
      price: "99",
      priceCurrency: "USD",
      availability: "https://schema.org/InStock",
      url: "https://twiceover.io/go/plan-premium",
    },
  ],
};

const groups = sectionGroups();
// One row of marks per section: included from the plan that adds it upward.
const includedOn = (section) => PLANS.map((plan) => PLANS.indexOf(plan) >= PLANS.indexOf(section.plan));
---
<Base
  title="Pricing — TwiceOver"
  description="One simple monthly subscription. No per-analysis charges. Use it with or without a broker connection."
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(productJsonLd)} />
  {/* The band (ADR 0912 ruling 1) — ink graphite, NOT the accent: a full-bleed
      non-interactive band painted in accent.default spends the scarcity that makes the two
      CTA borders and the mark read as actions. Its fill and two ink values are the dark
      theme's own bg.canvas / text.primary / text.secondary, taken by scoping
      data-theme="dark" to this section — the mechanism .trust-plate already uses on the
      landing page (ADR 0035) — rather than by copying three hexes in. No token is minted. */}
  <section class="pricing-band" data-theme="dark">
    <div class="container">
      <h1 class="pricing-band__title type-display">Simple subscription. You hold the decisions; we do the math.</h1>
      <p class="pricing-band__sub">One simple monthly subscription. No per-analysis charges. Use it with or without a broker connection — connecting read-only unlocks reads of your own positions against your own rules.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <ul class="tier-grid">
    <li class="tier">
      {/* Figure block (ADR 0912 ruling 2): eyebrow, price, cap figure over a border.strong
          hairline, and NO tinted fill — the hairline is the whole separation. The tier label
          stays an <h2> for the document outline; `.tier__eyebrow` is what gives it the
          eyebrow treatment the artifact draws with a bare <p>. */}
      <div class="tier__figures">
        <h2 class="tier__eyebrow">Free</h2>
        <p class="tier__price">$0</p>
        {/* The space between the two spans is load-bearing for TEXT, not layout: the 10px gap is
            flex `gap`, so without it the cap copies and reads out as "10analyses a month". A
            whitespace-only node between flex items is not rendered, so the visual is unchanged. */}
        <p class="tier__cap"><span class="tier__cap-n">10</span> <span class="tier__cap-w">analyses a month</span></p>
      </div>
      <p class="tier__line">Your Portfolio in full. The Outlook, built on fundamentals, technicals, peers, the sector and macro.</p>
      <div class="tier__foot">
        <a class="btn-quiet tier__cta" href="/go/try">Run a free analysis</a>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li class="tier">
      <div class="tier__figures">
        <h2 class="tier__eyebrow">Core</h2>
        <p class="tier__price">$49<span class="per">/mo</span></p>
        <p class="tier__cap"><span class="tier__cap-n">200</span> <span class="tier__cap-w">analyses a month</span></p>
      </div>
      <p class="tier__line">Everything in Free, plus: news and market voices, earnings and dividends, what options price in, and short interest.</p>
      <div class="tier__foot">
        <a class="btn-quiet tier__cta" href="/go/plan">Start 14-day trial</a>
        <p class="tier__disclosure">Free for 14 days. Then $49/month, charged automatically to the card you save at checkout.</p>
        <p class="tier__disclosure">Cancel any time before the trial ends — one click in Settings, no charge. We email you three days before.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li class="tier">
      <div class="tier__figures">
        <h2 class="tier__eyebrow">Premium</h2>
        <p class="tier__price">$99<span class="per">/mo</span></p>
        <p class="tier__cap"><span class="tier__cap-n">300</span> <span class="tier__cap-w">analyses a month</span></p>
      </div>
      <p class="tier__line">Everything in Core, plus: the largest options trades, earnings history against the implied move, and insider buying and selling.</p>
      <div class="tier__foot">
        <a class="btn-quiet tier__cta" href="/go/plan-premium">Start 14-day trial</a>
        <p class="tier__disclosure">Free for 14 days. Then $99/month, charged automatically to the card you save at checkout.</p>
        <p class="tier__disclosure">Cancel any time before the trial ends — one click in Settings, no charge. We email you three days before.</p>
      </div>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p class="mor-line">Subscriptions are sold by <strong>Dodo Payments</strong>, our merchant of record; TwiceOver is the software and service you're using. Prices in USD. You can cancel anytime; see the <a href="/refunds">Refund Policy</a>.</p>

  {/* Section-by-plan table (ADR 0912 Amendment 1 ruling 2). The mark is a plain ink dot — not a
      tick (ADR 0912 bars ticks as a scorecard read) and not the accent (navy marks actions only).
      Dot and dash are decoration; the visually-hidden word is what a screen reader hears. */}
  <section class="plans" aria-labelledby="plans-title">
    <h2 id="plans-title" class="plans__title">What each plan includes</h2>
    <p class="plans__lead">Your Portfolio comes with every plan. The plans differ in which sections of the analysis they include.</p>
    <div class="plans__table-wrap">
      <table class="plans__table">
        <caption class="visually-hidden">Sections of the analysis included on each plan</caption>
        <colgroup><col />{PLANS.map(() => <col class="plans__plan-col" />)}</colgroup>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Section</th>
            <th scope="col">Free<span class="plans__price">$0</span></th>
            <th scope="col">Core<span class="plans__price">$49/mo</span></th>
            <th scope="col">Premium<span class="plans__price">$99/mo</span></th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        {groups.map((group) => (
          <tbody>
            <tr class="plans__group">
              <th scope="rowgroup" colspan={PLANS.length + 1}>{group.label}<span class="plans__count">{group.sections.length}</span></th>
            </tr>
            {group.sections.map((section) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{section.name}<span class="plans__tag">{section.tagline}</span></th>
                {includedOn(section).map((included) =>
                  included ? (
                    <td><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="visually-hidden">Included</span></td>
                  ) : (
                    <td><span class="none" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="visually-hidden">Not included</span></td>
                  ),
                )}
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        ))}
        <tfoot>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Sections</th>
            {PLANS.map((plan) => <td>{sectionsOn(plan).length}</td>)}
          </tr>
        </tfoot>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="plans__foot">Each plan's Outlook weighs only the sections that plan shows.</p>
    <p class="plans__foot">Every figure in a section carries its source and the exact time it was fetched.</p>
  </section>
</Base>
